# a-base-drawn-in-tight — `EXPLORATION.md` § The order of work phase
# 2, `PROGRESSION.md` § P7 and `BACKLOG.md` E2: **the same defences,
# the same wave list, arranged two ways.**  This is the COMPACT
# column — the whole defence drawn in tight around the core.
#
# ⚠⚠ **Nothing in the corpus as it stood — 67 scenarios — varied the
# LAYOUT while holding the defences equal**, so `PROGRESSION.md`
# § P7's racing line — *a compact base is fast to service and
# concentrates the approach fan; a spread base covers more ground and
# costs travel time* — was a claim with no number under it.  These
# four files are the number:
#
#   a-base-drawn-in-tight.keys        wall+crew ±5,  towers ±4    170
#   a-base-drawn-out-wide.keys        wall+crew ±14, towers ±13   207
#   a-tight-base-with-no-towers.keys  wall+crew ±5,  no towers    102
#   a-wide-base-with-no-towers.keys   wall+crew ±14, no towers    102
#
# ⚠⚠ **THE LAYOUT IS WORTH +37 TICKS ON A BASE THAT LIVES 170 — AND
# THE SAME MOVE WITHOUT TOWERS IS WORTH EXACTLY NOTHING.**  102 against
# 102, to the tick: moving the whole front line nine hexes outward,
# eight wall hexes and both crew with it, buys ZERO.  ⚠ So what a
# layout is worth is not where the WALL is; it is **how far from the
# core the fight happens**, and a base with nothing that shoots has no
# such distance to sell.
#
# ⚠⚠ **And the towers were never the variable**: all four cells burn
# **all 30 shots each**, so the sixty shots are identical and only their
# GEOMETRY moved.  A swept 2x2 (uncommitted) separates the two halves
# and they are additive — wall+crew ±5 / towers ±13 and wall+crew ±14 /
# towers ±4 both read **187**, exactly halfway.
#
# Held equal, by construction and each for a reason a measurement
# already paid for:
#
#   * ⚠⚠ **the TOTAL walk** — the spawn markers sit at ±24 and the core
#     at (0, 0) in every file, so a robot walks 24 hexes to the core
#     whichever one it is in.  Moving the front outward shortens the
#     approach to it by exactly what it lengthens the walk behind it,
#     and the two cancel.  **A pair that did not null this would be
#     measuring the ROAD and calling it the layout** — and the
#     no-tower pair is what proves the cancellation is exact.
#   * ⚠⚠ **the wall's FACE WIDTH** — five rows with a gate on row 0, on
#     each front, in every file.  `@M020`: the siege front IS the
#     wall's width, so a wider wall would be a WALL result in a LAYOUT
#     costume.
#   * ⚠ **the wall's SHAPE** — a fixed-`q` column zigzags on odd-r
#     (`@M061`), and the same four rows give the same crinkle at any
#     `q`, so BRACING is identical.  `plans/14` H2 measured a 99-tick
#     artefact that read exactly like a finding.
#   * ⚠ **the FOOTING** — one palette kind everywhere.  `footing_of`
#     takes the sturdiest ground in REACH, so brittleness is a property
#     of a region (`@M061`).
#   * ⚠⚠ **the LIST RAMPS** — a flat list saturates the front and every
#     column reads the same (`@M085`, `@M087`).

echo a base drawn in tight: wall and crew at 5, towers at 4 — the COMPACT column

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# One tower per front, and OFF the gate lane — row -1 east, row +1
# west, so the base has no mirror to be read as one.
do cycle_kind
click 4 -1
click -4 1
marker 4 -1 tower
marker -4 1 tower

# ── Two spawn markers, at ±24 ───────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ Both are active every wave: `spawn_wave` round-robins over
# `active_spawn_markers`, so the list arrives half on each front.  ⚠⚠
# A one-front base is `CLAUDE.md`'s *a gate whose reading is already
# saturated* — `plans/14` H2 measured a second crew member beside the
# first at worth NOTHING and the same person on the other front at +28.
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 24 0
marker 24 0 spawn 3
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click -24 0
marker -24 0 spawn 0

count markers 5 5
do toggle_mode

# The road, one kind end to end.  ⚠ An unpainted hex IS sea and the
# markers at ±24 have to be stood on.
palette 5
drag -26 -2 26 -2
drag -26 -1 26 -1
drag -26 0 26 0
drag -26 1 26 1
drag -26 2 26 2
count painted 265 265

# ── The defence: eight wall hexes and two gates ─────────────────
#
# ⚠ The gate is not decoration and it is not generosity: `plans/12` B7
# measured a sealed wall at nearly double the clock and one with a gap
# in it at NOTHING — so the body standing in the gap IS the front, and
# it is also the only way the player gets out (`plans/13` V2).
palette 9
click 5 -2
click 5 -1
click 5 1
click 5 2
click -5 -2
click -5 -1
click -5 1
click -5 2
kind 5 0 grass
kind 5 1 wall
kind -5 0 grass
kind -5 1 wall

# One crew member in each gate.  ⚠ They are ORDERS (`helper_drive`),
# which `wave_assign` does not overrule (`@X296`) — a `.keys` file says
# *stay* by sending somebody somewhere.
crew 5 0
send 0 5 0
helper 0 5 0
crew -5 0
send 1 -5 0
helper 1 -5 0

park 0 0
wallet 200.0 200.0

schedule 12 12 12 16 20 24 30 36
waves 0 0
count alive 0 0

# ── The poke, and the drive home ────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ Out along row 0 through the east gate to the marker and back.  A
# vehicle covers TWO hexes a tick and skips every odd one, so 24 is
# reachable and 23 would not be.
drive 24 0
tick 13
vehicle 24 0
waves 1 1

drive 0 0
tick 13
vehicle 0 0

fall 900
ticks 166 174
